>
<h3 id=3D"hash-function">Hash function</h3>
<p>Hash function is used to hash a long URL to a short URL, also known as <=
em>hashValue</em>.</p>
<h4 id=3D"hash-value-length">Hash value length</h4>
<p>The <em>hashValue</em> consists of characters from [0-9, a-z, A-Z], cont=
aining 10 + 26 + 26 =3D 62 possible characters. To figure out the length of=
 <em>hashValue</em>, find the smallest <em>n</em> such that <em>62^n =E2=89=
=A5 365 billion</em>. The system must support up to 365 billion URLs based =
on the back of the envelope estimation. Table 1 shows the length of <em>has=
hValue</em> and the corresponding maximal number of URLs it can support.</p=
>
<table><thead><tr><th><strong>n</strong></th><th><strong>Maximal number of =
URLs</strong></th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td>1</td><td>62^1 =3D 62</td></t=
r><tr><td>2</td><td>62^2 =3D 3,844</td></tr><tr><td>3</td><td>62^3 =3D 238,=
328</td></tr><tr><td>4</td><td>62^ 4 =3D 14,776,336</td></tr><tr><td>5</td>=
<td>62^5 =3D 916,132,832</td></tr><tr><td>6</td><td>62^6 =3D 56,800,235,584=
</td></tr><tr><td><strong>7</strong></td><td><strong>62^7 =3D 3,521,614,606=
,208 =3D ~3.5 trillion</strong></td></tr><tr><td>8</td><td>62^8 =3D 218,340=
,105,584,896</td></tr></tbody></table>
<p class=3D"tableCaption">Table 1</p>
<p>When <em>n =3D 7, 62 ^ n =3D ~3.5 trillion</em>, 3.5 trillion is more th=
an enough to hold 365 billion URLs, so the length of <em>hashValue</em> is =
7.</p>
<p>We will explore two types of hash functions for a URL shortener. The fir=
st one is =E2=80=9Chash + collision resolution=E2=80=9D, and the second one=
 is =E2=80=9Cbase 62 conversion.=E2=80=9D Let us look at them one by one.</=
p>
<h4 id=3D"hash--collision-resolution">Hash + collision resolution</h4>
<p>To shorten a long URL, we should implement a hash function that hashes a=
 long URL to a 7-character string. A straightforward solution is to use wel=
l-known hash functions like CRC32, MD5, or SHA-1. The following table compa=
res the hash results after applying different hash functions on this URL:<b=
r>
<code>https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Systems_design</code></p>
<div class=3D"table-wrap" style=3D"--table-min-width: 500px;"><table><thead=
><tr><td><strong>Hash function</strong></td><td><strong>Hash value (Hexadec=
imal)</strong></td></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td>CRC32</td><td>5cb54054</td><=
/tr><tr><td>MD5</td><td>5a62509a84df9ee03fe1230b9df8b84e</td></tr><tr><td>S=
HA-1</td><td>0eeae7916c06853901d9ccbefbfcaf4de57ed85b</td></tr></tbody></ta=
ble></div>
<p class=3D"tableCaption">Table 2</p>
<p>As shown in Table 2, even the shortest hash value (from CRC32) is too lo=
ng (more than 7 characters). How can we make it shorter?</p>
<p>The first approach is to collect the first 7 characters of a hash value;=
 however, this method can lead to hash collisions. To resolve hash collisio=
ns, we can recursively append a new predefined string until no more collisi=
on is discovered. This process is explained in Figure 5.</p>
<figure class=3D"py-1"><div style=3D"display: flex; justify-content: center=
